.TH EXPAND 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
expand \- convert tabs to spaces
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B expand
.RB [ \-t
.IR tablist ]
.RI [ file ]\ .\|.\|.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B expand
copies each
.I file
(or standard input, if none is given) to standard output, replacing
each <tab> character with the number of <space> characters needed to
reach the next tab stop.
.PP
Input is read and copied one byte at a time, so there is no limit on
the length of an input line.
.PP
Each <backspace> character is copied to the output unchanged, and
decrements the column position used for tab stop calculations; the
column position is never decremented below the first column.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-t " tablist"
Set the tab stops used for the conversion, instead of the default of
every 8th column.
.I tablist
may be given as a separate argument
(\fB\-t\fR \fItablist\fR)
or attached directly to the option
(\fB\-t\fR\fItablist\fR).
.I tablist
is either:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
a single positive decimal integer, in which case tab stops are set
that many columns apart; or
.IP \(bu 2
two or more positive decimal integers, separated by <comma> or <blank>
characters, given in strictly ascending order, in which case tab stops
are set at exactly those column numbers. Once the last listed column
is passed, no further tab stops exist and any remaining <tab> is
replaced by a single <space>.
.RE
.IP
A
.I tablist
that is empty, contains a non-numeric or non-positive value, or is not
in strictly ascending order (when more than one value is given) is
rejected as invalid.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
A pathname of an input file. If
.B \-
is given, standard input is used at that point in the operand
sequence.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All input files were copied successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An input file could not be opened; the remaining operands are still
processed.
.TP
.B 2
An option or its argument was invalid.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR unexpand (1),
.BR tabs (1),
.BR cat (1)
